<Layout
  title="Research | Md. Farhatul Abrar"
  description="Research by Md. Farhatul Abrar on reactive molecular simulation, hydrothermal conversion of waste and biomass, life-cycle assessment, and process systems engineering."
>
  <main class="page">
    <h1>Research</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      My research asks why some waste-conversion processes perform better
      than others, and how that understanding can guide better process
      design. I work on hydrothermal conversion of waste and biomass, assess
      it with life cycle and techno-economic analysis, and am building
      toward molecular-level modeling of reaction mechanisms and kinetics. I
      aim to extend this toward the catalytic conversion of biomass and
      waste-derived feedstocks.
    </p>

    <section class="theme">
      <h2>Molecular Simulation &amp; Reaction Chemistry</h2>
      <p>
        Statistical optimization and life cycle assessment can show which
        conditions work better, but not why. To study the chemistry
        directly, I am using reactive molecular dynamics (LAMMPS with
        ReaxFF). I built a 2,232-atom cellobiose&ndash;water system matching
        experimental hydrothermal carbonization conditions and have completed
        500 ps simulations at 180&nbsp;&deg;C and 240&nbsp;&deg;C, with
        preliminary bond-order analysis of C&ndash;O bond dynamics and
        possible hydrolysis-related transformations. This work is at an early
        stage. My goal is to connect molecular-level mechanisms with reaction
        kinetics and process-scale models, and to build the computational
        foundations for studying catalytic reaction pathways. This direction
        also builds on my teaching of Reaction Engineering, which covers
        chemical kinetics and heterogeneous catalysis.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theme">
      <h2>Hydrothermal Conversion of Waste &amp; Biomass</h2>
      <p>
        I work on hydrothermal conversion of waste-derived feedstocks,
        including hydrothermal carbonization (HTC) and liquefaction (HTL). In
        my M.Sc. research on anaerobic digestate, I ran HTC experiments,
        characterized the hydrochar, and used Response Surface Methodology to
        optimize temperature (180&ndash;240&nbsp;&deg;C) and residence time
        (30&ndash;90&nbsp;min) for hydrochar yield and heating value. These
        objectives pull in opposite directions, so the optimization had to
        balance them. The joint optimum was near 190&nbsp;&deg;C and
        90&nbsp;min, giving a hydrochar yield of 64.2% and a heating value of
        18.9&nbsp;MJ/kg.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theme">
      <h2>Life Cycle &amp; Techno-Economic Assessment</h2>
      <p>
        I use life cycle assessment (LCA) to compare conversion pathways and
        identify their environmental hotspots. For anaerobic digestate, I
        compared open dumping, land application, composting, and HTC, using
        Monte Carlo simulation to test how uncertain inventory data affect
        the ranking. HTC cut climate change impact by 70% relative to open
        dumping. In a collaboration with Florida Institute of Technology, I
        conducted the LCA component of a combined techno-economic and life
        cycle study of hydrochar production from waste corn stover. It showed
        40.7% lower greenhouse gas emissions than open-field burning and
        identified the HTC reactor as the main hotspot. I have also
        contributed LCA studies on municipal solid waste, phase-change
        materials on biochar, and hydrogels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="theme">
      <h2>Process Systems Engineering</h2>
      <p>
        I am interested in evaluating process configurations under competing
        objectives. I have worked on process-network generation and modular
        LCA for comparing production pathways, and on a framework for a
        biorefinery process repository and superstructure generation to
        support LCA-based decision making (in preparation). Earlier, I
        modeled heat transfer and airflow in a natural-convection solar dryer
        using CFD in OpenFOAM, which was my introduction to computational
        modeling.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

